From 67bb5ee4f8bff8fba65e74e5c68ab63fa6f400af Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ZhangDM-520 <jixianqiang1540@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:33:12 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] feat(render): average the texel footprint when the scene
 minifies

A minified source (a render_scale supersampled client, a scaled-down
window, a thumbnail) was sampled with one bilinear fetch per output
pixel: at 3:1 that reports one pure texel and drops the other two,
aliasing text and fine detail the supersample just paid for.

Add a box (area) downsample as the default method: the tex shader
gains a downsample variant whose taps tile the fragment's whole texel
footprint at ratio/K texel steps (exact one-texel centers while
integer ratios fit the constant 4x4 grid; the capped grid still spans
the footprint). Ratios derive from the transformed destination
extents, so rotated draws keep their axes. Taps work on
GL_TEXTURE_EXTERNAL_OES, where mipmap filtering is unavailable.

The method is a scene policy ([appearance] downsample = "box" |
"bilinear" | "nearest", default "box") applied to every minified
scene buffer; buffers that pin WLR_SCALE_FILTER_NEAREST stay exact and
sources that are not minified are untouched. A cropped minified source
clamps its whole footprint. A reload re-renders through a new
sceneDownsample change flag. The damage path's existing
non-integer-ratio expansion already bounds the box footprint
(ceil(ratio)/ratio output pixels per axis), so its margin is
unchanged.

Pins: config_load and config_change units; render/downsample_box
screenshots a per-texel column pattern at 3:1 and 5:1 and asserts the
box, bilinear, and restored-after-reload pixels (mutation-proven:
scene policy, reload application, the tap loop, and the capped step
each go red).

Co-authored-by: Copilot <223556219+Copilot@users.noreply.github.com>
